        blurb: 'The 20 amino acids are the alphabet of proteins. You don\'t need to memorize all 20 at once — group them by property. In bioinformatics, you\'ll see their 1-letter codes constantly in protein sequences.',
        detail: `<strong>Nonpolar / Hydrophobic</strong> — fold to inside of proteins:\n• G — Gly — Glycine (smallest; allows tight turns)\n• A — Ala — Alanine\n• V — Val — Valine\n• L — Leu — Leucine\n• I — Ile — Isoleucine\n• P — Pro — Proline (creates kinks in chain)\n• F — Phe — Phenylalanine (large aromatic ring)\n• W — Trp — Tryptophan (largest; only 1 codon = UGG)\n• M — Met — Methionine (start amino acid; AUG codon)\n\n<strong>Polar / Uncharged</strong> — can H-bond, often on protein surface:\n• S — Ser — Serine\n• T — Thr — Threonine\n• C — Cys — Cysteine (-SH group; forms disulfide bonds)\n• Y — Tyr — Tyrosine (can be phosphorylated)\n• N — Asn — Asparagine\n• Q — Gln — Glutamine\n\n<strong>Positively charged (Basic)</strong> — attract DNA and negative molecules:\n• K — Lys — Lysine (histone proteins are lysine-rich; acetylation target)\n• R — Arg — Arginine (most positive; DNA-binding)\n• H — His — Histidine (imidazole ring; pH-sensitive; enzyme active sites)\n\n<strong>Negatively charged (Acidic)</strong>:\n• D — Asp — Aspartate\n• E — Glu — Glutamate\n\n<strong>Essential amino acids</strong> (9 — must eat, can\'t synthesize): His, Ile, Leu, Lys, Met, Phe, Thr, Trp, Val\nMemory: "PVT TIM HaLL" = Phe, Val, Thr, Trp, Ile, Met, His, Leu, Lys\n\n<strong>In bioinformatics</strong>: protein sequences are written with 1-letter codes. BLAST output shows alignments like:\nQUERY: MGLSDGEWQLVLNVWGKVEADIPGHG...\nSBJCT: MGLSDGEWQLVLNVWGKVEA.IPHH...\nEach letter = one amino acid.`,
